From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:25:20 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] PROTOTYPE pvr: cache load-op and end-of-tile shaders per
 device

Local experiment for amazon-suez, never to be sent (Imagination declines
LLM-generated code in src/imagination).

pvr compiled a load-op shader (pvr_uscgen_loadop) for every
vkCmdBeginRendering that loads or clears, and an end-of-tile shader
(pvr_usc_eot) for every render. In phoc on zink, during a panel gesture on
GX6250, those were 8 % and 7-9 % of the compositor's CPU (perf). Both
depend only on a few inputs, so keep the compiled binaries in per-device
hash tables, in memory:

* load ops: keyed by the clear/load/2D-view/MSAA masks, destination
  formats, depth-clear register and the MRT setup fields the generator
  reads. Not the whole usc_mrt_resource: mrt_desc's valid_mask and
  priority hold uninitialised words beyond the target's size (seen on
  the board as ASCII fragments), which made every key unique.
* end of tile: keyed by the props, including the PBE state words (render
  target addresses) and tile buffer addresses; at most 128 entries,
  emptied when full.

SUEZ_PVR_NO_LOADOP_CACHE / SUEZ_PVR_NO_EOT_CACHE disable them;
SUEZ_PVR_CACHE_DEBUG prints hits and misses. In phoc over one gesture:
44/44 load-op and 43/44 end-of-tile lookups hit; gles-layers check passes
(16 and 40 stripes).

Generated-by: LLM
